 90s kind of have a chance to write  those wrongs  um so that was kind of the that was the  core kind of idea behind  using the actors over and over again and  you know there's little things like  characters that live  in 1666 die in the other movies or  characters that die in the earlier ones  live in this one  so there was like a whole kind of thing  of like history repeating itself and  again things writing right wrongs being  righted  was there any particular character that  that appears in multiple films that  maybe was a little more challenging to  to craft that through line for  well i mean i think that an interesting  one is actually jeremy who plays  peter in 94 kind of sam's like douchebag  boyfriend  and then he plays caleb in 1666 because  you know it was it was easier with the  ones that um  that we kind of like that we feel like  we understand and we're rooting for and  then we have these other guys who are  just like  there and we're kind of like why do you  exist there so he was challenging  um i really love kind of the dichotomy  between mad thomas  in 1666 and tommy in 1978 and mccabe  mccabe who plays both of those he shot  matt thomas first and he was so amazing  as mad thomas that i kind of was  inspired  um to to change a little bit of how his  character was um  was portrayed in the in the second movie  the nightwing killer  used to be masked very early  in 1978 and then mccabe was just so  amazing  that i was like why are we putting a  mask on you 20 pages in  let's like watch you be tommy let's  watch you  become this awful monster and then we'll  get to the mask at the end and so that  was really motivated by how amazing he  was  very very effective choice also in  78 you had a lot of brutal kill  sequences  leading up to this but i was very  curious about your choice  to sit on the like the stabby stabbiness  of it for so so long like it's very  effective  but i was like wow like you're really  going for it with this one  yeah i um i was okay so i shot that  movie last  that was the last movie in the three and  i was so  tired i was so tired by the end that i  was like  more blood more blush more violence  i'm not kidding it was just like the the  wardrobe the special effects people the  costume they would like  they would all come up to me and i'd be  like just more just make it more  and it was just like a real reflection  of where my soul was at the time  i feel like that serves the film quite  well totally it's so brutal  and awful and um it's i think it's  really good but but that was a lot to  have that had to do with it  yeah all right going to solomon now  actually more specifically sarah i was  wondering if if solomon hadn't killed  the widow  and taken the book and let's say sarah  was the one who found the book  do you think that she actually would  have went through it or would she have  found maybe a different way to get back  at the folks in the town  yeah i think that she i don't think she  would have ultimately gone through with  it i think that obviously we never got  to that point but i think that like  she was obviously motivated very much by  her love for  for hannah and trying to save her and  trying to find a way out and i think  that  her kind of kiana speech that she gives  us sarah where she's like  it doesn't matter if we're guilty or not  they think we are and that's all  like that's very real but i think that  she would have cont i think she has that  spirit of dina in her  of sarah and her she would have  continued to rage and fight even though  she's brought to the edge of kind of  like  am i going to make this deal am i not so  yeah i feel it watching it  and now obviously i've had even more  time to obsess over the opening credits  of 94 and the one that caught my eye  there was the humpty dumpty killer  like who is that and why did that  individual not appear in the film  i don't know i love the humpty dumpty  killer he was in the original script  like i put him in the